### What is the autoboxing trap?

`List<Short>` (and `List<Integer>`) has two `remove` methods:
- `remove(int index)` removes by position.
- `remove(Object o)` removes by value.

When you call `list.remove(1)`, the literal `1` is an `int`. Java picks `remove(int index)` because an exact `int` overload exists. It does NOT autobox `1` to `Short` or `Integer`.

### How does the trap manifest?

```java
package com.bank.util;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class AutoboxingTrapDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Short> list = new ArrayList<>();

        for (short i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            list.add(i);  // autoboxes short to Short
            if (list.size() > 1) {
                list.remove(i);  // TRAP: i is widened to int!
                // This calls remove(int index), NOT remove(Short value)
            }
        }

        System.out.println(list);
    }
}
```

The `remove(i)` call removes the element AT INDEX `i`, not the element WITH VALUE `i`. This causes `IndexOutOfBoundsException` when `i` exceeds the list size.

### How do you fix it?

```java
// CORRECT: remove by VALUE using explicit boxing
list.remove(Short.valueOf(i));       // remove the Short with value i

// CORRECT: remove by VALUE using cast
list.remove((Short) i);              // cast short to Short wrapper

// SAFEST: use removeIf -- no overload ambiguity
short target = i;
list.removeIf(x -> x == target);     // removes elements matching value
```

### How do you demonstrate the difference?

```java
List<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<>(List.of(10, 20, 30, 40));

// Remove by INDEX: removes element at position 1 (which is 20)
nums.remove(1);           // [10, 30, 40]

// Remove by VALUE: removes the Integer object 30
nums.remove(Integer.valueOf(30));  // [10, 40]
```

### What are the overload resolution rules?

| Argument Type | Method Called | Reason |
|---------------|-------------|--------|
| `int` literal (1) | `remove(int index)` | Exact match for int parameter |
| `short` variable | `remove(int index)` | short widens to int (widening > boxing) |
| `Short` object | `remove(Object o)` | Short is an Object |
| `Integer.valueOf(1)` | `remove(Object o)` | Integer is an Object |

Java's overload resolution prefers widening over boxing. A `short` widens to `int`, matching `remove(int index)`, rather than boxing to `Short` to match `remove(Object)`.

### What are common mistakes?

WRONG: Assuming `list.remove(i)` removes by value when `i` is `int`.

WRONG: Not realizing that `short` widens to `int` before overload resolution.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- This trap is specific to `List` because `List` inherits `remove(int index)` from `java.util.List` AND `remove(Object)` from `java.util.Collection`. The two overloads create the ambiguity.
- `Set.remove(Object)` has only one overload, so this trap does not apply to Sets.
- In production banking code, prefer `removeIf(predicate)` for value-based removal. It is clear, safe, and avoids the overload trap entirely.
- This is one of the top 5 Java interview trick questions alongside `==` vs `equals`, `String` pool, `finally` with `return`, and `ConcurrentModificationException`.
